course: dictionary
lesson: 10897
title: Virtually: almost, online, or as good as
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Virtually means almost, or very nearly but not quite. The first thing to check is what is missing. «The jar was virtually empty» means there is a tiny bit left, but almost nothing.
explain: Virtually also means done on a computer or online, not in real life. «We met virtually on a video call» means you saw each other through a screen, not in the same room.
explain: In formal writing, virtually means "as good as". «It is virtually impossible» means it can hardly ever happen. «Virtually all» means almost all, not every single one, so do not treat it as an exact number.
fact: Virtually means almost, or very nearly but not quite.
fact: Virtually can mean done on a computer or online, as in a virtual tour or virtual lessons.
fact: In formal sentences, virtually can mean "as good as": «virtually impossible» means it can hardly ever happen.
fact: Virtually softens big words like all, every, nothing and impossible.
fact: To check the meaning, swap in "almost" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The jar was virtually empty.» What does virtually mean here? | *Almost empty, with just a tiny bit left | Very heavy | Completely full | Broken into pieces
exercise: Ella says, «We met virtually for our lesson.» What does virtually tell you? | *They met online, through a screen | They met by accident | They met in the same classroom | They met on a train
exercise: «It is virtually impossible to finish all the reading in one minute.» What does virtually mean in this sentence? | *As good as impossible, it can hardly ever happen | Very slow and boring | Done in a computer game | Quite possible, if you hurry
test: Which word could replace virtually in «The shop was virtually empty»? | *Almost | Never | Yesterday | Loudly
test: A virtual tour of a museum means... | Walking round the museum with a guide | *Looking round it on a computer or screen | Closing the museum for the day | Buying a ticket at the door
exam: «Virtually all the class passed the test.» What does virtually show about all? | Exactly every child | No children | Only one child | *Almost every child, but not quite all
